Q: Nested flexbox items shrink at a weird ratio (or don&#39;t grow) A: <h1>Summary (Cause and Solution)</h1>
<h2>Cause</h2>
<p>In a flex container, width is ignored when flex properties are involved, and only .c had a defined flex-basis (700px) while .a did not. This caused .c to participate in flex sizing while .a remained a fixed-size item, so the flexbox algorithm could not make them equal in size.</p>
<h2>Solution</h2>
<p>Apply compatible flex values to both .a and .c so they participate equally in the flex sizing algorithm. By giving them the same flex shorthand (<code>flex: 0 1 20%</code>), both elements share the same basis and shrink behavior, allowing flexbox to size them equally while letting the remaining space be distributed to the other items.</p>
<pre class="lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>.a {
  flex: 0 1 20%;
}
.c {
  flex: 0 1 20%;
}
.b, .inner {
  flex: 1 0 60%;
}
.flex {
  display: flex;
}
div {
  min-width: 0;
  outline: 1px solid black;
}
</code></pre>
<h2>One-line version</h2>
<p>The original code mixed width and flex-basis, so only one item participated in flex sizing; assigning matching flex values to both items makes them equal-sized under the flexbox algorithm.</p>

Q: Uncaught (in promise) ZodError A: <p>I had the same problem with the recent version of Zod, and the solution I found was:</p>
<pre><code>import { z } from 'zod';

/**
 * Custom Zod resolver for React Hook Form that safely handles validation errors
 * without throwing uncaught promise exceptions.
 *
 * @param schema - Zod schema to validate against
 * @returns Resolver function compatible with React Hook Form
 *
 * @example
 * ```tsx
 * const schema = z.object({ name: z.string().min(1) });
 * const form = useForm({ resolver: safeZodResolver(schema) });
 * ```
 */
export const safeZodResolver = &lt;T extends z.ZodType&lt;any, any&gt;&gt;(schema: T) =&gt; {
    return async (data: any, _context: any, _optionss: any) =&gt; {
        try {
            const result = await schema.safeParseAsync(data);

            if (!result.success) {
                return {
                    values: {},
                    errors: result.error.issues.reduce((acc: any, issue) =&gt; {
                        const path = issue.path.join('.');
                        if (!acc[path]) {
                            acc[path] = {
                                type: issue.code,
                                message: issue.message,
                            };
                        }
                        return acc;
                    }, {}),
                };
            }

            return {
                values: result.data,
                errors: {},
            };
        } catch (error) {
            console.error('Validation error:', error);
            return {
                values: {},
                errors: {
                    root: {
                        type: 'manual',
                        message: 'Form error validation',
                    },
                },
            };
        }
    };
};
</code></pre>
<pre><code>And then you use it in:

Before: const resolver = zodResolver(schema);
After: const resolver = safeZodResolver(schema);
</code></pre>
